<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>

<!--
	The period control that the tiles and charts of one tab read.
	
	Two components, one channel. The right one holds the period itself - two date fields, always
	visible, always editable - and the left one is a list of quick ways to fill them. A reader who
	picks "30 Tage" therefore sees which thirty days those are and can drag one end of the range
	without first switching into some other mode. What stands in the two fields is what every panel
	of the tab counts.
	
	A <selector> is the framework's own component for "pick one value and publish it", and it is what
	makes a preset a single click. The two dates live in a <settings> form beside it - the
	framework's own component for "edit a transient object and hand it to dependent components",
	built exactly the way the engine's demo builds one
	(com.top_logic.demo/technical/components/transientObjects/settingsForm.layout.xml is a von/bis
	form of this shape).
	
	The channel that every panel of the tab reads is the form's model, not the selector's selection.
	That is the whole trick: the selector fills the form, the form owns the value, and a panel still
	reads one channel carrying one period. Nothing downstream has to know how the two days were
	arrived at; it asks the period for its window and its label and gets them
	(see tl.ai.monitor:Period, where those are derived attributes).
	
	The traffic runs both ways, and has to. Picking an option writes two days into the form; editing
	a day by hand and pressing the check mark writes "Benutzerdefiniert" back into the selector. It
	must never be possible for the selector to read "30 Tage" while the fields say something else -
	that is the same lie as a tile whose caption has drifted from the number beside it.
	
	The options are plain day counts, with 0 for "the whole span the data covers" and -1 for "typed
	in by hand". They are deliberately not a model enum: the option set is a property of this view,
	and a classifier would have to be migrated into every application that ships this monitor.
	
	Neither component has a business model of its own - the options are a constant and the form model
	is made here - and the selector says so through supportsModel. That is not cosmetic: a selector
	observes every type, and with the default "everything is a possible model" it would invalidate
	itself on every single agent run.
	
	One control per tab, not one per panel. Five period pickers on one screen that can disagree with
	each other would be worse than the fixed windows they replace.
-->
<template>
	<params>
		<param name="selectorName"/>
		<param name="rangeName"/>
	</params>

	<layout name="${rangeName}Row"
		horizontal="true"
		resizable="false"
	>
		<layoutInfo size="98px"/>
		<components>
			<selector name="${selectorName}"
				alphabeticalOrder="false"
				securityObject="securityRoot"
				showMaximize="false"
				showMinimize="false"
				showPopOut="false"
				titleKey="admin.ai.agentActivity.period.label"
				toolbar="true"
			>
				<layoutInfo size="300px"/>
				<optionBuilder class="com.top_logic.model.search.providers.ListModelByExpression"
					elements="model -> list(1, 7, 30, 90, 0, -1)"
					supportsModel="model -> isEmpty($model)"
				/>
				<optionLabelProvider class="com.top_logic.model.search.providers.LabelProviderByExpression">
					<label><![CDATA[
						period -> ifElse($period < 0,
							#('Custom'@en, 'Benutzerdefiniert'@de).localize(),
							ifElse($period == 0,
								#('All'@en, 'Alle'@de).localize(),
								ifElse($period == 1,
									#('1 day'@en, '1 Tag'@de).localize(),
									#('{0} days'@en, '{0} Tage'@de).fill($period).localize())))
					]]></label>
				</optionLabelProvider>
				<!--
					30 days, so that a reader who does not touch the control sees what the charts
					showed when their period was fixed. The tiles used to count 24 hours and now
					start at 30 days too - two panels sharing one control cannot keep two different
					defaults, and the trend charts are the panels whose period a reader is more
					likely to have in mind.
				-->
				<defaultSelectionProvider class="com.top_logic.model.search.providers.ListSelectionProviderByExpression"
					function="options -> 30"
				/>
			</selector>

			<settings name="${rangeName}"
				securityObject="securityRoot"
				showMaximize="false"
				showMinimize="false"
				showPopOut="false"
				titleKey="admin.ai.agentActivity.period.range.title"
				toolbar="true"
			>
				<!--
					The period itself, remade whenever the selector changes. A fresh object rather
					than a mutated one on purpose: a channel notifies its readers when its value
					changes, and a value that is the same object as before has not changed.
					
					Every branch fills both days - that is what makes a preset nothing more than a
					quick way of writing two dates. "N Tage" is the N days before today plus today,
					which is the range the trend charts have drawn under that option all along.
					"Alle" reads the oldest and the newest run there is, so that even the unbounded
					option says over what it is counting; it is the one branch that has to touch the
					table, and it does so once per click on that option rather than on every read.
					Its upper end is at least today, so that a run recorded with a future date - a
					clock skew, an import - cannot fall outside "Alle".
					
					"Benutzerdefiniert" picked from the list is the only branch that fills nothing
					new: it keeps the days that are on screen, so switching to it changes the label
					and not the numbers. It is reached from the list only when a reader wants the
					preset name gone; the ordinary way in is to edit a day and press the check mark.
				-->
				<model class="com.top_logic.model.search.providers.TransformLinkingByExpression"
					input="selection(${selectorName})"
				>
					<function><![CDATA[
						selected -> old -> {
							preset = ifElse(isEmpty($selected), 30, $selected);
							today = now().roundDay();
							daysBack = n -> $today.toSystemCalendar().withDayAdded(-$n).toDate().roundDay();
							from = ifElse(isEmpty($old), null, $old.get(`tl.ai.monitor:Period#fromDay`));
							to = ifElse(isEmpty($old), null, $old.get(`tl.ai.monitor:Period#toDay`));
							period = new(`tl.ai.monitor:Period`, transient: true);
							$period.set(`tl.ai.monitor:Period#preset`, $preset);
							ifElse($preset > 0, {
									$period.set(`tl.ai.monitor:Period#fromDay`, $daysBack($preset));
									$period.set(`tl.ai.monitor:Period#toDay`, $today);
								},
								ifElse($preset == 0, {
										starts = all(`tl.ai.execution:AgentExecution`)
											.map(e -> $e.get(`tl.ai.execution:AgentExecution#startedAt`))
											.filter(d -> !isEmpty($d))
											.sort();
										$period.set(`tl.ai.monitor:Period#fromDay`,
											ifElse(isEmpty($starts), $today, $starts.firstElement().roundDay()));
										$period.set(`tl.ai.monitor:Period#toDay`,
											ifElse(isEmpty($starts), $today, {
												newest = $starts.lastElement().roundDay();
												ifElse($newest.isDateGreater($today), $newest, $today);
											}));
									},
									{
										$period.set(`tl.ai.monitor:Period#fromDay`,
											ifElse(isEmpty($from), $daysBack(30), $from));
										$period.set(`tl.ai.monitor:Period#toDay`,
											ifElse(isEmpty($to), $today, $to));
									}));
							$period;
						}
					]]></function>
				</model>
				<!--
					The two days stand side by side at every width. The type's own form has two
					columns too, but the framework stacks them below a width it decides, and this row
					is one line high: at 1440 px with the navigation open, "Bis" was in a second line
					nobody could see (#866). A columns layout without line break keeps both fields in
					the row; where the row is narrower than the two, it scrolls sideways rather than
					hiding the second one. That layout sets its fields 8px lower than a stacked one,
					which is why the row is 98px high and not 86px.
				-->
				<modelBuilder class="com.top_logic.element.layout.formeditor.builder.EditFormBuilder">
					<forms>
						<form type="tl.ai.monitor:Period">
							<formDefinition>
								<columnsLayout
									columns="TWO"
									lineBreak="false"
								>
									<field
										attribute="fromDay"
										fullQualifiedName="tl.ai.monitor:Period#fromDay"
										type="tl.core:Date"
									/>
									<field
										attribute="toDay"
										fullQualifiedName="tl.ai.monitor:Period#toDay"
										type="tl.core:Date"
									/>
								</columnsLayout>
							</formDefinition>
						</form>
					</forms>
				</modelBuilder>
				<buttons>
					<!--
						Applying is a copy, for the same reason the transform above builds a new
						object: the form writes its two fields into the model it was built from, so
						after form-apply the period is already correct but is still the same object,
						and the panels reading the channel would never hear about it. The copy is
						what makes the change a change.
						
						It is also where the period loses its preset name. Two days that a reader
						entered are no longer "die letzten 30 Tage", even when they happen to be the
						same two days, and a caption that still claimed so would be exactly the drift
						this control exists to prevent. Writing -1 back into the selector is what
						moves the visible option along with the value; when the selector already
						stands there it changes nothing, which is why the model is pushed as well.
					-->
					<button id="applyAgentActivityPeriod"
						class="com.top_logic.model.search.providers.CommandHandlerByExpression"
						group="System"
						image="css:fas fa-check"
						operation="period -> $period.copy(transient: true)..set(`tl.ai.monitor:Period#preset`, -1)"
						transaction="false"
					>
						<resourceKey key="admin.ai.agentActivity.period.range.apply"/>
						<postCreateActions>
							<setModel target="selection(${selectorName})">
								<input class="com.top_logic.model.search.providers.ValueTransformationByExpression"
									function="period -> -1"
								/>
							</setModel>
							<setModel target="model(self())">
								<input/>
							</setModel>
						</postCreateActions>
					</button>
				</buttons>
			</settings>
		</components>
	</layout>
</template>